;; Lifecycle: permanent. The memory of a program: one, which every module
;; shares. A module takes it from a module it requires with (memory from
;; name), down to this one, which defines it (spec 3.3) and holds nothing
;; of its own. Its layout: the bytes below heap-start are compiler/print.sub's,
;; which writes from them; from heap-start up lie every module's data, the
;; C language's heap and stack (base/lang.sub, sbrk) among them. It grows as
;; the program needs (memory.grow, from sbrk).

(provide heap-start)

(memory 1)

(global heap-start i32 1024)
